arXiv:2510.04488v1 [cs.AI] 06 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:4.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Agent Collaborative Intelligence: Dual-Dial Control for Reliable LLM Reasoning</span></p>
</div><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:487.8pt;"><span id="p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
<span id="p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="p2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="p2.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="p2.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Edward Y. Chang,
Ethan Y. Chang</span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:487.8pt;"><span id="p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
<span id="p3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="p3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="p3.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Stanford University, UIUC</span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-agent debate often wastes compute by using a fixed adversarial stance, aggregating without deliberation, or stopping on heuristics. We introduce <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math>, an active controller with two independent dials that decouple information from behavior: an information dial that gates evidence by quality, and a behavior dial that schedules contentiousness from exploration to consolidation. A moderator tracks disagreement, overlap, evidence quality, and argument quality, and halts when gains plateau. We provide theory-lite guarantees for nonincreasing dispersion and provable termination, with a budget-feasible scheduler. Across clinical diagnosis and news-bias tasks, <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math> improves accuracy and calibration while reducing tokens, and converts residual uncertainty into precision RAG plans that specify what to retrieve next. We use a cross-family LLM judge (<math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CRIT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CRIT</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CRIT}</annotation></semantics></math>) as a conservative soft weight and stop signal, validated for order invariance and judge-swap stability; stability depends on using high-capability judges. <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math> turns debate into a budget-aware, measurable, and provably terminating controller.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite intense interest, multi-agent debate has seen limited progress in theory or practice. Unmoderated or ad hoc collaboration lacks provable termination, calibrated uncertainty, and cost control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cemri et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Common approaches use a fixed adversarial stance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, aggregate without deliberation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, or stop on heuristics, which wastes compute and locks in early errors.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The control problem.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reliable multi-agent reasoning requires <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">active modulation</em>. Early rounds should explore alternatives, and later rounds should consolidate on well-supported conclusions. This demands two independent controls—what information enters (evidence gating) and how agents interact (behavioral stance)—together with principled stopping that detects convergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Beyond single-dial modulation.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work introduced contentiousness modulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang, 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang, 2023b</a>)</cite>, showing that behavior matters, but a single dial is insufficient. Systems need concurrent control over information quality and interaction intensity, and they must stop when additional debate yields diminishing returns. The <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CRIT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CRIT</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CRIT}</annotation></semantics></math> framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang, 2023a</a>)</cite> evaluates arguments but has not been coupled to systematic stopping.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math>: Dual dials with measured stopping.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math> (Multi-Agent Collaborative Intelligence), an active controller with two orthogonal dials. The <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">information</em> dial (<math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>) gates evidence by quality, and the <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">behavior</em> dial (<math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CL</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CL}</annotation></semantics></math>) schedules contentiousness from exploration to consolidation. A moderator tracks four signals—disagreement (<math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{JS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>JS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{JS}}</annotation></semantics></math>), overlap (<math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Overlap is not synonymous with agreement; it measures convergence of <em id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evidence</em>, whereas disagreement measures convergence of <em id="footnote1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">beliefs</em>. We require both.</span></span></span>, evidence quality (<math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>), and argument quality (<math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CRIT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CRIT</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CRIT}</annotation></semantics></math>)—and halts when gains plateau. Beyond prediction, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math> translates residual uncertainty into precision RAG: targeted acquisition plans that specify what to retrieve next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Guarantees and scheduler.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under gated averaging with smoothing, dispersion is nonincreasing and the plateau test terminates in <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, improving to <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log(1/\varepsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log(1/\varepsilon))</annotation></semantics></math> under a contraction condition (Lem. <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 (Monotonicity under gated averaging). ‣ M.2 Monotonicity of dispersion ‣ Appendix M Plateau Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Prop. <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2 (Termination in 𝑂(1/𝜀) expected rounds). ‣ M.4 Termination of the plateau controller ‣ Appendix M Plateau Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, Appx. <a href="#A13" title="Appendix M Plateau Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M</span></a>). A budget-feasible UCB scheduler over <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta\alpha,\Delta\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta\alpha,\Delta\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> achieves <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}(\sqrt{KT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(\sqrt{KT})</annotation></semantics></math> no-regret with zero expected budget violation (Prop. <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (No-regret versus best fixed schedule). ‣ L.4 Main guarantee ‣ Appendix L Learned Scheduler for (𝛼,𝛾)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Appx. <a href="#A12" title="Appendix L Learned Scheduler for (𝛼,𝛾)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">L</span></a>).</p>
</div>
