</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results #3: Signal convergence and behavioral transfer.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Debates average <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.4</annotation></semantics></math> rounds and stop when dispersion and information gains plateau. Convergence traces mirror G1; see Appx. <a href="#A10.SS3" title="J.3 Supplemental: News Bias Detection and Mitigation with MACI ‣ Appendix J News Bias Detection and Mitigation with MACI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J.3</span></a> for signal plots.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04488v1/DR-Bias-Ratings.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:639/208;" width="639" height="208" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Convergence signals during bias mitigation (news bias). Wasserstein distance falls, normalized MI rises and then plateaus, and cross-entropy declines. Debates stop when dispersion and information gains plateau, mirroring the clinical setting (Appx. <a href="#A10.SS3" title="J.3 Supplemental: News Bias Detection and Mitigation with MACI ‣ Appendix J News Bias Detection and Mitigation with MACI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J.3</span></a>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Example (one case).</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On “Christopher Columbus,” one agent begins Euro–centric (expansion and trade) and the other foregrounds indigenous harms. Over four rounds the former acknowledges severe consequences within broader colonial policy and recommends balanced presentation with citations (Table <a href="#A10.T20" title="Table 20 ‣ J.3.4 Bias mitigation, arguments, and convergence traces ‣ J.3 Supplemental: News Bias Detection and Mitigation with MACI ‣ Appendix J News Bias Detection and Mitigation with MACI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>, Appx. <a href="#A10.SS3" title="J.3 Supplemental: News Bias Detection and Mitigation with MACI ‣ Appendix J News Bias Detection and Mitigation with MACI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Portability.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Same code and hyperparameters, no domain tuning. Controls (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CRIT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CRIT</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CRIT}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> gates, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CL</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CL}</annotation></semantics></math> scheduling, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{JS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>JS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{JS}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}</annotation></semantics></math> stopping) transfer across evidence types and label structures. Per–article distributions appear in Appx. <a href="#A10.SS3" title="J.3 Supplemental: News Bias Detection and Mitigation with MACI ‣ Appendix J News Bias Detection and Mitigation with MACI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Budget-aware learned scheduler.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also provide a drop–in scheduler that replaces the fixed <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> schedule with a small bandit over <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta\alpha,\Delta\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta\alpha,\Delta\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> moves, optimizing per–round IG gains and dispersion drops under a token budget. The policy enjoys <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}(\sqrt{KT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(\sqrt{KT})</annotation></semantics></math> no–regret against the best fixed schedule with zero expected budget violation (Prop. <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (No-regret versus best fixed schedule). ‣ L.4 Main guarantee ‣ Appendix L Learned Scheduler for (𝛼,𝛾)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Appx. <a href="#A12" title="Appendix L Learned Scheduler for (𝛼,𝛾)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">L</span></a>). We keep the fixed schedule in G1/G2 main tables for comparability.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Advancing multi-agent orchestration.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math>, a control framework that extends contentiousness modulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang (2023a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang (2023b)</a></cite> with (1) <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dual-dial</em> control separating information admission from behavioral stance, (2) <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">information-theoretic</em> stopping via relative progress ratios, and (3) <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adaptive</em> initialization to reduce wasted exploration. Unlike passive aggregation or fixed debate, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math> steers deliberation using measurable signals—disagreement, overlap, evidence quality, and argument quality—and halts when gains plateau.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On clinical diagnosis (1,500 cases), <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math> improves accuracy (+3.9 pp over majority vote; +3.7 pp over fixed-contentiousness) and calibration (ECE <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.081" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.081</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.081</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.103" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.103</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.103</annotation></semantics></math>) while using <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19\%</annotation></semantics></math> fewer generation tokens; case studies show convergence (disagreement <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>86–96%, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CRIT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CRIT</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CRIT}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>21–26%). <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CRIT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CRIT</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CRIT}</annotation></semantics></math> is order-invariant and stable under cross-family judge swaps (2–3% winner flips; Appx. <a href="#A5.SS7" title="E.7 CRIT Reliability and Role in MACI ‣ Appendix E CRIT: Cross-Family Argument Quality Evaluator" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.7</span></a>). Theory-lite guarantees bound dispersion and ensure termination in <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> rounds (improving to <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log(1/\varepsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log(1/\varepsilon))</annotation></semantics></math>), and a budget-feasible UCB scheduler attains <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}(\sqrt{KT})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}(\sqrt{KT})</annotation></semantics></math> no-regret with zero expected budget violation (Appx. <a href="#A13" title="Appendix M Plateau Guarantees" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">M</span></a>, <a href="#A12" title="Appendix L Learned Scheduler for (𝛼,𝛾)" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">L</span></a>). News-bias validation (619 articles) shows portability: the same controls reduce the partisan gap by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="68\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>68</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">68\%</annotation></semantics></math> without domain tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations and directions.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.0em;">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scope.</span> We evaluate two domains with subjective judgment; broader testing on tasks with objective ground truth (math, code, factual QA) and longer chains remains future work. Retrieval quality mediates performance (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 G1: Clinical Diagnosis ‣ 4 Empirical Validation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), suggesting gains from stronger evidence acquisition.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-judge dependence.</span> <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CRIT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CRIT</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CRIT}</annotation></semantics></math> uses cross-family evaluators and may inherit bias. We mitigate via identity masking, span-grounded justifications, and judge-swap checks (2–3% flips; Appx. <a href="#A5.SS7" title="E.7 CRIT Reliability and Role in MACI ‣ Appendix E CRIT: Cross-Family Argument Quality Evaluator" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.7</span></a>). Lower-capability regimes may require larger <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, tighter stop tolerances, and periodic human audits.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost trade-offs.</span> <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math> uses <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.7{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.7{\times}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens vs. single-model inference; while accuracy gains justify overhead for complex cases, domain-specific cost–benefit studies are needed.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameters.</span> <math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CL</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CL}</annotation></semantics></math> schedules, gates, and plateau tolerances are tuned on dev data; generalization beyond diagnosis/news is open. Scaling to 3–5 agents and meta-learned initialization are natural next steps.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Closed-loop RAG.</em> A promising direction is coupling <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math>’s acquisition plans to budgeted RAG with end-to-end evaluation; transcripts (Appx. <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Detailed MACI Case Study: Dengue vs. Chikungunya Diagnosis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>, <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Extended MACI Case Study #2: Hepatitis Differential Diagnosis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>) enable auditability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MACI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MACI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MACI}</annotation></semantics></math> reframes multi-agent debate as a controllable, auditable, and <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">provably terminating</em> controller, turning orchestration from heuristics into a budget-aware, measurable process.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
