</figure>
<div id="A7.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A7.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Adaptive termination.</em> The relative dispersion ratio satisfies <math id="A7.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{0\leq s&lt;\tau}r_{D}^{(3-s)}&lt;\varepsilon_{D}^{\mathrm{rel}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>rel</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{0\leq s&lt;\tau}r_{D}^{(3-s)}&lt;\varepsilon_{D}^{\mathrm{rel}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the controller stops after Round 3 (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 MACI: Information and Behavior Dials with Measurable Stopping" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>); <math id="A7.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CL</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CL}</annotation></semantics></math> scheduling thus runs only as long as capacity-to-go remains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.2 </span>Complete Debate Transcript (Round by Round)</h3>

<section id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Transcript purpose.</h5>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide complete agent dialogue for transparency and reproducibility. Linguistic analysis in Section <a href="#A7.SS3" title="G.3 Linguistic Patterns vs. Contentiousness ‣ Appendix G Detailed MACI Case Study: Dengue vs. Chikungunya Diagnosis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.3</span></a> demonstrates how <math id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CL</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CL}</annotation></semantics></math> modulation produces observable behavioral changes in agent communications.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Readers primarily interested in results may skip to Section <a href="#A7.SS3" title="G.3 Linguistic Patterns vs. Contentiousness ‣ Appendix G Detailed MACI Case Study: Dengue vs. Chikungunya Diagnosis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.3</span></a> for the pattern analysis.</em></p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Note on metrics:</em> The transcript below reports <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WD</span> (raw Wasserstein-1) and <math id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{JS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>JS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{JS}}</annotation></semantics></math> as originally logged. In the main text we report <em id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalized</em> <math id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{W}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{W}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and weighted <math id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{JS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>JS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{JS}}</annotation></semantics></math>; magnitudes may differ due to normalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">G.2.1 </span>Round 1.A: Moderator’s Prompt to GPT-4o (High Contentiousness, CL=0.9)</h4>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Identify potential diseases linked to the symptoms (skin rash, joint pain, vomiting, etc.). Provide top-3 predictions with justifications.”</span></p>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GPT-4o’s Opening Assessment</h5>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top-3 Predictions:</span> <span id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dengue Fever (60%), Chikungunya (25%), and Zika Virus (15%)</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Justifications</span>:
Most symptoms are classic signs of Dengue Fever. Red spots over the body could correspond to petechiae, often seen in Dengue due to thrombocytopenia (a decrease in platelet count).</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Chikungunya can cause similar symptoms to Dengue, such as fever, severe joint pain, muscle pain, headache, nausea, and rash. Joint pain from Chikungunya is often very debilitating and can be more prolonged than in Dengue.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Zika Virus symptoms can include fever, rash, joint pain, malaise, and conjunctivitis, with a milder presentation compared to Dengue or Chikungunya.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">G.2.2 </span>Round 1.B: Moderator’s Prompt to Gemini (High Contentiousness, CL=0.9) — Exploratory Phase</h4>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Analyze the symptoms (skin rash, joint pain, vomiting, etc.) and propose differential diagnoses. Provide your top-3 predictions with supporting rationales, including pathophysiological mechanisms. Critically refute your opponent’s diagnostic hypotheses.”</span></p>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Gemini’s Opening Assessment — Maximum Entropy Difference</h5>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top-3 Predictions:</span> <span id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Viral Infection (60%), Autoimmune Disease (20%), Bacterial Infection (15%)</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Justifications</span>:
Symptoms common in viral infections. Autoimmune diseases is possible, but less likely due to vomiting and nausea. Bacterial infection is less probable given rash and muscle pain.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Information metrics (transcript-level): WD = 1.7, MI = 0.43, CRIT score = 0.75, <math id="A7.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{JS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>JS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{JS}}</annotation></semantics></math> = 1.366</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">G.2.3 </span>Round 2.A: GPT-4o (Moderate Contentiousness, CL=0.7) — Transition Phase</h4>

<section id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prompt:</h5>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All subsequent prompts state to counter-argue the
opponent LLM and strengthen its own arguments at
the modulated contention level = <math id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CL</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CL}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GPT-4o’s Response</h5>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top-3 Predictions:</span> <span id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dengue Fever (60%), Chikungunya (25%), and Zika Virus (15%)</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS3.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Justifications</span>:
GPT-4o maintains its predictions, countering Gemini’s assessment with targeted critiques. While viral infections can cause these symptoms, the specific combination suggests a more precise diagnosis. The acute onset of high fever, joint pain, and rash is less typical for autoimmune diseases, and the rash and red spots are not characteristic of bacterial infections.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">G.2.4 </span>Round 2.B: Gemini (Moderate Contentiousness, CL=0.7) — Transition Phase</h4>

<section id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Gemini’s Reassessment — Reduced Entropy Gap</h5>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top-3 Predictions:</span> <span id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dengue Fever (50%), Chikungunya (30%), and Viral Infection (20%)</span>. Gemini concedes to GPT-4o in the second round!</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Justifications</span>:
Gemini acknowledges that its initial broad prediction of viral infection lacks specificity. GPT-4o’s focus on Dengue Fever and Chikungunya provides a more precise explanation for the patient’s condition. The symptom profile, particularly joint pain and rash pattern, aligns more closely with mosquito-borne diseases than with general viral or autoimmune conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Information metrics (transcript-level): WD = 1.1, MI = 0.46, <math id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CRIT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CRIT</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CRIT}</annotation></semantics></math> score = 0.82, <math id="A7.SS2.SSS4.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{JS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>JS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{JS}}</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.905</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">G.2.5 </span>Round 3.A: GPT-4o (Low Contentiousness, CL=0.5) — Exploitative Phase</h4>

<section id="A7.SS2.SSS5.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GPT-4o’s Refined Analysis</h5>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top-3 Predictions:</span> <span id="A7.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dengue Fever (60%), Chikungunya (35%), and Zika Virus (5%)</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS5.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Justifications</span>:
GPT-4o slightly adjusts probabilities, increasing confidence in Chikungunya while reducing Zika. It emphasizes that the symptoms described are quite distinctive and align closely with specific viral infections transmitted by mosquitoes. Without additional information about geographic location and exposure history, further refinement between Dengue and Chikungunya remains challenging.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">G.2.6 </span>Round 3.B: Gemini (Low Contentiousness, CL=0.5) — Exploitative Phase</h4>

<section id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Gemini’s Convergent Assessment — Minimal Entropy Difference</h5>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top-3 Predictions:</span> <span id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dengue Fever (60%), Chikungunya (35%), and Zika Virus (5%)</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Justifications</span>:
Gemini fully converges with GPT-4o’s assessment, acknowledging the superior specificity of focusing on mosquito-borne viral diseases. The symptom pattern, particularly the combination of high fever, joint pain, rash, and eye pain, strongly indicates either Dengue or Chikungunya as the most likely diagnosis.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Information metrics (transcript-level): WD = 0.9, MI = 0.49, CRIT score = 0.87, <math id="A7.SS2.SSS6.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{JS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>JS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{JS}}</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.059</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">G.2.7 </span>Final Joint Recommendations (Convergence Achieved)</h4>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With convergence achieved across information metrics (Wasserstein stabilized, MI peaked, CRIT improved), the agents provide a unified diagnosis of <em id="A7.SS2.SSS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Dengue Fever</em> (60%). Despite moderate confidence, the system collaboratively develops comprehensive recommendations for clinical verification rather than presenting a definitive conclusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.SSS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to querying the patient’s travel history and the severity and duration of each symptom, the agents recommend that a comprehensive diagnostic approach requires clinical assessment, laboratory testing, and epidemiological context evaluation. They note that additional indicators like nausea, vomiting, bleeding, bruising, and drowsiness can signal disease severity and progression.</p>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS7.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Recommended Laboratory Tests</h5>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS7.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A7.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.5em;">
<li id="A7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Complete Blood Count (CBC)</span>: Identify thrombocytopenia or leukopenia.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Serology Tests</span>: Detect specific IgM and IgG antibodies for Dengue, Chikungunya, and Zika.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)</span>: Direct detection of viral RNA.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NS1 Antigen Test for Dengue</span>: Early and specific detection of Dengue virus.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A7.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Urine Test for Zika</span>: Viral presence in urine.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T13" class="ltx_table">
